        <section id="what-is-coding-language?"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>What is coding language?</h2>
              <p>Scroll through the slides below to learn more about what coding language is and what is it made up of.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Characters and words
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Much like the everyday languages that we are used to communicating in, 'coding' involves the use of characters and words fashioned into groups to make clear what we are trying to communicate.</p>
<p>It uses a set of strings of these different 'characters' and/or 'words' organised in different ways and, again like our own spoken languages this will vary according to the coding language that is being used.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u1_s1_p3_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Constantly changing
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Coding language can also be paralleled once more, to language we all use and how it has evolved and changed from its origins. In the same way the ‘English language’ has developed from Latin and Greek to name but two.</p>
<p>Coding language is constantly changing, developing, and updating.</p>
<p>Coding languages can be high-level or low-level. High-level languages are easier for humans to understand and can run on most machines. In contrast, low-level languages are more difficult for humans understand and are more machine-specific as they control the hardware directly.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u1_s1_p3_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Different types of coding languages
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Mark-up languages: In the example of a webpage, these would specify the content, and the visual settings. Most commonly used are HTML and CSS.</p>
<ul>
<li>HTML – contains blocks of text and identifies what they are (e.g. headers/body text)</li>
<li>CSS – specifies the visual parameters for the HTML content (e.g. size, shape, position)</li>
</ul>
<p>Programming languages: For example JavaScript – mediates user interactions (e.g. what happens when you click on something/access to backend software)</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u1_s1_p3_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
